# Root-cause and systemic architectural remediation

Follow the mandatory [common AI-assisted development governance protocol](../../docs/engineering/AI_ASSISTED_DEVELOPMENT_GOVERNANCE.md#22-mandatory-root-cause-and-systemic-architectural-remediation) across Cursor, ChatGPT, Codex and delegated coding agents.

- Identify the intended behavior, symptom, evidence and causal mechanism before implementing a defect correction. Distinguish confirmed, candidate and unresolved causes.
- Search for recurring failure mechanisms, sibling implementations, shared contracts, pending work and affected consumers; identify applicable invariants and existing enforcement. Report the searched scope and remaining unknowns.
- Prefer the smallest sound durable correction at the highest justified causal layer. Reuse canonical implementations and prevention mechanisms; avoid repetitive symptom patches and speculative refactoring.
- Obtain appropriate authorization before material scope expansion or architectural/contract changes. Coordinate affected agents and shared consumers. Propose missing invariants through existing ADR and backlog governance.
- If urgent stabilization is justified, preserve mandatory invariants and required gates; explicitly track residual systemic risk and follow-up in the existing backlog. A mitigation is not closure of the systemic cause.
- Verify the causal correction with independently justified regression and negative controls, including affected siblings and consumers as appropriate. Preserve test integrity and scoped verification budgets.
- Report the confirmed cause, affected scope, correction, verification evidence and unresolved risks separately from symptom resolution. Never claim class-wide prevention without evidence.

The canonical document defines the full procedure. This rule does not authorize new product/customer remediation actions, uncontrolled cross-component changes, comprehensive master CI or automatic merges. It is mandatory procedural guidance, not an executable enforcement mechanism.
